1. Login and Identity Check
Only people from trusted government or police departments can log in.

They use their email, password, and sometimes an OTP (code) for extra safety.

We make sure the email belongs to a valid organization (like @cybercrime.gov.in).

2. Role-Based Access
Every user has a specific role:

Admin – Can see everything, including passwords.

Forensic Analyst – Can upload and view files, but not see the password.

Observer – Can only view reports and logs.

This keeps everything secure and legal.

3. Uploading the Locked File
The user uploads a password-protected file (like a ZIP, PDF, DOCX, etc.).

The system checks what type of file it is.

4. Trying to Unlock the File
Based on the file type, we pick the right tool:

fcrackzip for ZIP files

pdfcrack for PDFs

John the Ripper for many types

msoffcrypto for Word/Excel files

The system then tries to guess the password using:

A list of common passwords

Trying every possible password (brute-force)

AI that predicts possible passwords based on file name or user details

5. Viewing the Decrypted File
If the password is found:

The system opens the file safely inside the browser.

Admins can download or export the file.

Others can only view the content inside a protected viewer.

This keeps the password safe and prevents misuse.

6. Activity Tracking and Report Generation
Every action is recorded:

Who uploaded what

What was cracked

When and how it was viewed

A report is automatically created, showing:

File details

Cracking method

Password found (if allowed)

Time and date

These reports help in court or internal investigations.

7. Using AI to Help Crack Passwords
If AI is turned on:

The system uses clues from the file name or user info to guess smart passwords.

These guesses are tried first, saving time.

8. Safety Features
Files are deleted after use or timeout.

All passwords are encrypted before saving.

Repeated failed logins or suspicious behavior will block the user.

Every step is tracked with IP address and time.

